1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,331
This is Signal, Yara Alsinan's individual term project: a research assistant for

2
00:00:06,331 --> 00:00:09,497
understanding changes in company financial results.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,800
Understanding a company means more than looking at its stock price. People

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:19,600
need to compare financial results, find the right reporting periods, and check

5
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:24,401
the evidence behind an explanation. Signal aims to reduce that repeated work.

6
00:00:24,401 --> 00:00:29,201
It builds on Stock Sense by investigating the company behind the stock.

7
00:00:29,201 --> 00:00:34,001
The current prototype focuses on NVIDIA. Whether people find this useful enough

8
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:36,401
to return remains something to test.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,912
Streamlit turns Python code into the local web interface. Python selects financial

10
00:00:44,912 --> 00:00:49,823
facts and calculates changes. The data comes from saved SEC company facts

11
00:00:49,823 --> 00:00:54,735
and a historical NVIDIA filing. Claude Haiku four point five, accessed through

12
00:00:54,735 --> 00:00:59,646
MIT Parley, selects tools and explains their results. This uses an existing

13
00:00:59,646 --> 00:01:04,558
language model; no custom model is trained. Opening the dashboard makes no

14
00:01:04,558 --> 00:01:07,423
model request. Clicking Research uses Parley credits.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,741
The first important code path is fact selection. This excerpt checks the

16
00:01:14,741 --> 00:01:19,482
filing accession, start date, end date, and form, within the requested financial

17
00:01:19,482 --> 00:01:24,223
unit. It requires exactly one matching observation. That avoids silently mixing quarterly

18
00:01:24,223 --> 00:01:28,964
revenue with six month cash flow. Python then calculates percentage changes and

19
00:01:28,964 --> 00:01:33,705
operating margins. The selected periods both contain one hundred eighty two days.

20
00:01:33,705 --> 00:01:36,866
Reported rounded earnings per share are explicitly labeled.

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,495
The second code path is the agent loop. This shortened excerpt shows

22
00:01:44,495 --> 00:01:48,991
the model request and tool dispatch. The model can request the financial

23
00:01:48,991 --> 00:01:53,486
comparison or a curated management passage. Tool results are returned to the

24
00:01:53,486 --> 00:01:57,982
model as evidence. The loop stops after at most four model requests

25
00:01:57,982 --> 00:02:02,477
and six tool calls. Source IDs must have been retrieved before the

26
00:02:02,477 --> 00:02:06,972
final answer cites them. This checks references, but does not prove every

27
00:02:06,972 --> 00:02:08,096
sentence is supported.

28
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,158
Here is the working Streamlit interface, captured from the app. Users see

29
00:02:15,158 --> 00:02:20,316
summary cards and choose among cash flow, operating profitability, and earnings per

30
00:02:20,316 --> 00:02:25,474
share. The snapshot date is visible. This walkthrough uses screenshots of existing

31
00:02:25,474 --> 00:02:29,342
results, rather than presenting a new live model request.

32
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,918
The facts table is populated directly from Python, independently of AI prose.

33
00:02:33,918 --> 00:02:37,835
Revenue grew ninety five point eight percent, while operating cash flow grew

34
00:02:37,835 --> 00:02:41,753
seventy four percent. The gap is twenty one point nine percentage points.

35
00:02:41,753 --> 00:02:45,671
The table also shows period dates, units, margins, and the exact diluted

36
00:02:45,671 --> 00:02:46,324
share decrease.

37
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,675
Below the facts, the app separates the AI explanation from the calculations.

38
00:02:53,675 --> 00:02:57,350
This screenshot shows the saved earnings per share answer and its source

39
00:02:57,350 --> 00:03:01,025
reference. Expandable source sections expose the retrieved inputs and filing link. For

40
00:03:01,025 --> 00:03:04,700
cash flow, the evidence tool returns one curated management passage, not a

41
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:06,231
search of the entire report.

42
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,826
Testing exposed a real error: the model described a share decrease as

43
00:03:13,826 --> 00:03:17,652
twenty three million instead of two hundred thirty three million. Python now

44
00:03:17,652 --> 00:03:21,477
supplies the exact difference, and the interface separates facts from interpretation. Ten

45
00:03:21,477 --> 00:03:25,303
offline checks pass, but model explanations can still overreach. One company, one

46
00:03:25,303 --> 00:03:29,129
historical filing, and one curated passage limit coverage. Live accuracy and usefulness

47
00:03:29,129 --> 00:03:30,085
still require evaluation.

48
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,364
Before the final submission, the priorities are to retrieve more relevant evidence,

49
00:03:38,364 --> 00:03:41,728
evaluate the twelve reference tasks, add persistent research history, and implement reliable

50
00:03:41,728 --> 00:03:45,093
manual refresh. Broader company coverage and scheduled checks come after the core

51
00:03:45,093 --> 00:03:48,457
is reliable. The complete midpoint code, public snapshots, setup instructions, and limitations

52
00:03:48,457 --> 00:03:51,821
are on GitHub. Signal already demonstrates a working tool using research workflow;

53
00:03:51,821 --> 00:03:54,064
the next goal is making it more dependable.
